# Shell scripts keep LF endings on every platform.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on a default Windows install, so without this rule
# git rewrites `.githooks/pre-commit` to CRLF on checkout. The kernel then
# reads the shebang as an interpreter literally named `/bin/sh\r`, which does
# not exist. That matters more than the usual line-ending pedantry: a
# pre-commit hook that stops running does not announce itself, so the failure
# mode is a repository that silently loses its guard against committing a
# credential -- which is the exact accident the hook was written for.
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/** text eol=lf
